    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": "\n \n                         VETO MESSAGE - No. 149\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 7492, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend the social services law, in relation to coverage\n      and billing procedures in the Medicaid program for  complex  reha-\n      bilitation technology for patients with complex medical needs\"\n \nTO THE SENATE:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bills:\n \nSenate Bill Number 3421-A, entitled:\n \n    \"AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to making care\n      and  services  provided  by  licensed  mental health practitioners\n      eligible for coverage under the Medicaid program\"\n \nSenate Bill Number 5960, entitled:\n\n    \"AN ACT to amend the social services law, in relation to making care\n      and services provided by licensed clinical social workers eligible\n      for coverage under the Medicaid program\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  Assembly bill number 7492 would  amend  the  Social  Services  Law  to\nestablish  a new category of specialized durable medical equipment (DME)\nproviders to provide complex rehabilitation technology (CRT). The  Medi-\ncaid  program  would  be required to cover CRT products and services for\ncomplex needs patients, when  provided  by  such  Medicaid-enrolled  CRT\nsuppliers. The bill would also create a new standard for calculating the\nreimbursement  rate  for  CRT to ensure adequate access to such products\nand services.\n \n  Senate bill number 3421-A would  amend  the  Social  Services  Law  to\nauthorize  Medicaid  reimbursement  for  care  and  services provided by\nlicensed mental health counselors, marriage and family therapists, crea-\ntive arts therapists and psychoanalysts.\n \n  Senate bill number 5960 would amend the Social Services Law to author-\nize Medicaid reimbursement for care and services  provided  by  licensed\nclinical social workers.\n \n  While  I  find  the  intent of the legislature laudable in desiring to\nensure that individuals in the Medicaid program have access to appropri-\nate services and equipment, these bills would require the expenditure of\nfunds that are unbudgeted, and with Medicaid facing a considerable fund-\ning gap, should be considered during budget discussions.\n \n  With respect to Assembly bill number  7492,  this  proposal  has  been\nvetoed  previously  and  the  current  bill continues to raise concerns.\nFirst, the Medicaid program already covers CRT when it is deemed  to  be\nmedically  necessary and ensures access to such products and services by\n \nrequiring adequate reimbursement to CRT providers. Also, due  to  recent\nchanges  in  Medicare  at  the federal level, all Medicaid DME providers\nwill be required to satisfy the Medicare DME provider quality and train-\ning  standards.  Creating  a  new state-level provider category would be\nduplicative of the new federal standards and  would  create  significant\nadministrative costs to the Medicaid program.\n \n  With  respect to Senate bill numbers 3421-A and 5960, as drafted these\nbills inadequately describe the specific services to be  covered;  would\nrequire  significant clarification regarding the population groups to be\nserved; and do not specify the appropriate settings  in  which  services\nare  to  be provided in order to achieve consistency and quality in both\nmental and behavioral health services.\n \n  For these reasons, I am constrained to veto these bills.\n \n  The bills are disapproved.                  (signed) ANDREW M. CUOMO\n \n                               __________\n",
